WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.390
Pull a continent apart and the brittle upper crust breaks into

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:09.250
normal-fault blocks. Where neighboring faults dip in opposite directions, relatively high

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:12.770
blocks form horsts and downdropped blocks form grabens.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.330
The basins receive sediment from adjacent ranges.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:19.110
With time, erosion lowers the mountains and basin fill can overlap

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.130
their edges. Relative block height and fault geometry define the terms.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.420
If neighboring faults dip in the same direction, the blocks can

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.360
rotate like books tipping on a shelf.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.420
A lowered corner becomes a basin;

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.400
a raised corner becomes a ridge.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.740
Continued rotation tilts older beds more steeply than younger deposits and

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.600
can rotate faults to gentler dips.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.380
This arrangement accommodates much more extension than blocks that simply drop

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:45.200
without rotating.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.510
Extension is possible near a convergent boundary.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.030
It can thin the forearc, or stretch hot weak crust behind

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:55.830
the volcanic arc. Enough extension behind the arc creates a back-arc

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.530
ocean basin, with land-derived sediment near its edges and deep-ocean sediment

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:03.870
farther out. The location relative to the arc distinguishes forearc from

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.290
back-arc; extension alone does not tell you which one you are

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:07.510
seeing.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.360
The basin's history is written in its fill.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.180
Older units may have nearly uniform original thickness and then become

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:18.280
faulted and tilted. Deposits laid down during basin growth become thick

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.660
in the subsiding part and may contain coarse debris from steep

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.920
slopes. A younger flat unit that overlaps the basin and its

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.900
faults shows that this phase of basin formation had ended before

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:28.860
that unit was deposited.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:34.370
For a dated interval, average accumulation rate is deposited thickness divided

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:38.990
by elapsed time. In the textbook example, rapid accumulation after about

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.730
fifteen million years ago helps identify basin initiation.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.730
That date belongs to this example, not all rift basins.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.530
Combine dates, thickness changes, tilting, and overlap relationships;

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.230
a thick layer alone cannot establish when extension began.
